#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316987 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f8e49a0c-9575-5bd0-af4e-6463495e56b3" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating a Sum of Functions With Constant Factors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e91d8c0-34da-51b2-b77b-8ac14c502e44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x^2-6x+24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316989}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e91d8c0-34da-51b2-b77b-8ac14c502e44" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3x^2-6x+24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316989} :answer-field/id #uuid "4326c645-1ad3-5aed-8f60-91004839b56b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e14d24a3-1cd9-526f-8184-e87b4d2a71dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-128282" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+24x-7.$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate the function, applying the addition, constant multiple, and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+24x-7) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{x}^{3})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-3{x}^{2})+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(24x)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-7) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{3})-3\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})+24\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(x)-7\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\  & =-3{x}^{(3-1)}-3\\cdot 2{x}^{(2-1)}+24\\cdot 1-7\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =-3{x}^{2}-6x+24\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316990 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4bd2a8e-357a-5f22-bd50-608e68432a8d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Critical Points of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "64b48d89-f704-50a0-b695-a0d1df18cae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "64b48d89-f704-50a0-b695-a0d1df18cae4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316992} :answer-field/id #uuid "985dcd21-b215-519c-80c9-77d60646aaae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab2fd3ad-2935-5251-acc3-c0f04970e089" :question/math-academy-id "q-128283" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+24x-7.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-3x^2-6x+24$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nIf $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2}$ are the critical points of $f(x),$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "A critical point of $f(x)$ is a point $x=c$ in the domain of $f$ such that either ${f}^{′}(c)=0$ or ${f}^{′}(c)$ does not exist.\n\nPreviously, we found that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-3{x}^{2}-6x+24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now proceed to find the critical points:\n\n* First, we compute the points where ${f}^{′}(c)=0.$ We set the derivative equal to zero, and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3{x}^{2}-6x+24 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has stationary points at $x=-4$ and $x=2.$ \n\n* Second, we note that ${f}^{′}(x)$ exists for all values of $x$ in the domain of $f(x).$ So $f(x)$ has no non-stationary critical points.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the critical points of $f(x)$ are $x={x}_{1}=-4$ and $x={x}_{2}=2.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=-4\\cdot 2=-8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "1671fb4f-937f-5a3c-85b7-7f54f35ea543" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Increasing Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0087ccd5-dcb0-5d7b-8820-be57e0c99862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316995}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0087ccd5-dcb0-5d7b-8820-be57e0c99862" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316995} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d1250b7-66d6-5af7-83c7-c9546a0af4a5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316994} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fd3f7411-25a8-5305-af02-9886f74fc428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd3f7411-25a8-5305-af02-9886f74fc428" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316997} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2006850-9c49-5299-ae93-b099ef7d5a37" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316996}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4bec7a07-28c1-5fb2-9ffa-37e07c5a537a" :question/math-academy-id "q-128284" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+24x-7.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-3x^2-6x+24$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nIf $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2}$ are the critical points of $f(x),$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-8$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ is increasing for $x\\,\\in \\,($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,,\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,)$" :question/worked-solution "The function is increasing where ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$ \n\nPreviously, we found that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=-3{x}^{2}-6x+24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we need to solve the inequality\n\n$$\n-3{x}^{2}-6x+24>0.\n$$\n\nThe above inequality factors as\n\n$$\n-3(x+4)(x-2)>0.\n$$\n\nThe graph of $y=-3(x+4)(x-2)$ has roots at $x=-4$ and $x=2,$ as shown below:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14042687/assets/41fbc51820e147872fddfd0f124f0235a26513992f07a4b5681f2d8cb25d4a3b.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the function is increasing for $x\\in (-4,2).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186316998 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "33965139-0c6a-50fc-993a-e2acd6760212" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Local Minima and Local Maxima of Polynomials"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba381183-3e3b-51d3-be77-cd6419b2a4b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317000}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba381183-3e3b-51d3-be77-cd6419b2a4b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317000} :answer-field/id #uuid "647a8609-7527-55b9-b2cd-71b632319a41" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316999} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a62b386b-c4c9-5058-a7ce-e9e9f1ff1d14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317002}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a62b386b-c4c9-5058-a7ce-e9e9f1ff1d14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317002} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9c92eb8-a333-5cc2-8ca9-dc5a92bb494f" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "39a0f9d2-488f-5ef5-9f41-ec3876e3f6f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-128285" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+24x-7.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-3x^2-6x+24$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nIf $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2}$ are the critical points of $f(x),$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-8$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ is increasing for $x\\,\\in \\,($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,,\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,)$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFor which values of $x$ does the function $f(x)$ have local maxima and local minima?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \nis a local minimum\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \nis a local maximum" :question/worked-solution "We have the derivative \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-3{x}^{2}-6x+24=-3(x+4)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the two critical points $x=-4$ and $x=2.$ We use the first derivative test to determine whether these points are local maxima or minima.\n\nLet's choose the test points $x=-5,0,3$ and evaluate the first derivative at these points.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(-5) & =-3(-5+4)(-5-2)=-21<0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(0) & =-3(0+4)(0-2)=24>0 \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-3(3+4)(3-2)=-21<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe summarize the information in the table shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(-\\infty ,-4)$ | $-4$ | $(-4,2)$ | $2$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $-5$ |  | $0$ |  | $3$ |\n| ${f}^{′}$ | $-$ | $0$ | $+$ | $0$ | $-$ |\n| $f$ | $↘$ | min | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\n \nFinally, let's use the table above to classify the critical points.\n\n* At $x=-4,$ the first derivative changes sign from negative to positive. Therefore, $x=-4$ is a local minimum.\n\n* At $x=2,$ the first derivative changes sign from positive to negative. Therefore, $x=2$ is a local maximum."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317003 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2106ea68-f48f-5372-87c0-fa8126509655" :knowledge-point/title "Discarding Stationary Points of a Function on a Given Interval"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6062ed2b-60d6-5936-9849-bdcbaec7496a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317005}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6062ed2b-60d6-5936-9849-bdcbaec7496a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317005} :answer-field/id #uuid "263011a6-ff78-52b0-87cb-aa146aff6f08" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317004} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7307c708-581e-50d6-9246-e400bd16fd6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317007}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7307c708-581e-50d6-9246-e400bd16fd6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-33" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317007} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a1b0ad1-f5e7-5c2f-be0f-74d14e923194" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6bbdc17e-2e75-51ae-9305-829559459a78" :question/math-academy-id "q-128286" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+24x-7.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-3x^2-6x+24$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nIf $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2}$ are the critical points of $f(x),$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-8$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ is increasing for $x\\,\\in \\,($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,,\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,)$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nFor which values of $x$ does the function $f(x)$ have local maxima and local minima?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \nis a local minimum\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \nis a local maximum\n\nCurrent part:\n\nWhat are the global maximum and global minimum values of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-1,3]?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The global maximum is ${f}_{max}\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The global minimum is ${f}_{min}\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let's apply the candidates test step by step.\n\n**Step 1**: We already know the critical points of the function:\n\n$$\nx=-4,\\,x=2.\n$$\n\nSince $x=-4$ does not belong to the interval $[-1,3]$, the critical points on the interval $[-1,3]$ are $x=2,$ and the endpoints $x=-1$ and $x=3$ (since the derivative is not defined at the endpoints).\n\n**Step 2**: We evaluate $f(x)$ at the critical points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-1) & =-(-1)^{3}-3(-1)^{2}+24(-1)-7=-33 \\\\ f(2) & =-(2)^{3}-3(2)^{2}+24(2)-7=21 \\\\ f(3) & =-(3)^{3}-3(3)^{2}+24(3)-7=11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: We compare all of the values found in step 2.\n\n* The greatest value is $f(2)=21,$ so the global maximum is ${f}_{max}=21.$\n\n* The smallest value is $f(-1)=-33,$ so the global minimum is ${f}_{min}=-33.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317008 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f65f58-bd3d-5c3f-b9e0-462d74d75b59" :knowledge-point/title "Computing Second Order Derivatives"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ea35c20e-3191-5d4c-9ee4-ecbd0609144b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ea35c20e-3191-5d4c-9ee4-ecbd0609144b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6x-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317010} :answer-field/id #uuid "480bc046-ad06-54f6-88de-96fa7428328c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317009}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52649331-ed07-5264-902f-852819d58cf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-128287" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+24x-7.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-3x^2-6x+24$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nIf $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2}$ are the critical points of $f(x),$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-8$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ is increasing for $x\\,\\in \\,($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,,\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,)$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nFor which values of $x$ does the function $f(x)$ have local maxima and local minima?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \nis a local minimum\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \nis a local maximum\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nWhat are the global maximum and global minimum values of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-1,3]?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The global maximum is ${f}_{max}\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $21$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The global minimum is ${f}_{min}\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-33$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{″}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{″}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We already know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =-3{x}^{2}-6x+24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the second derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & ={(-3{x}^{2}-6x+24)}^{′} \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 2x-6\\cdot 1+0 \\\\  & =-6x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317011 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b47fa40d-613a-5e9b-ba07-c9057dd1a96e" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intervals on Which a Function Is Concave Up or Down"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa9986dc-a3c0-5669-9d86-e42ed676ba5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317013}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa9986dc-a3c0-5669-9d86-e42ed676ba5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317013} :answer-field/id #uuid "352eb4a6-711a-507f-b9e0-3c204e4ffeff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317012} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f547b8cd-6cdb-51eb-ad16-8aa503ef4824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f547b8cd-6cdb-51eb-ad16-8aa503ef4824" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317015} :answer-field/id #uuid "2477825b-c62a-5f79-b50f-8c66c1437a78" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317014}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac6f6022-abc0-588d-90bb-509620c29298" :question/math-academy-id "q-128288" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+24x-7.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-3x^2-6x+24$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nIf $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2}$ are the critical points of $f(x),$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-8$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ is increasing for $x\\,\\in \\,($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,,\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,)$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nFor which values of $x$ does the function $f(x)$ have local maxima and local minima?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \nis a local minimum\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \nis a local maximum\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nWhat are the global maximum and global minimum values of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-1,3]?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The global maximum is ${f}_{max}\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $21$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The global minimum is ${f}_{min}\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-33$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{″}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{″}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-6x-6$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nDetermine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $x\\,\\in \\,($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\,,\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$)$" :question/worked-solution "A function $f(x)$ is concave up on an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{″}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nEarlier, we found that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =-6x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find where the function is concave up, we solve the inequality ${f}^{″}(x)>0,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6x-6 & >0 \\\\ -6x & >6 \\\\ x & <-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is concave up on $(-\\infty ,-1)$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "841151db-1c26-5059-9796-75b70e41f659" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Points of Inflection Using Differentiation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "903a26d3-c572-5a1e-b71c-879c002a2f14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "903a26d3-c572-5a1e-b71c-879c002a2f14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317018} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac666c59-d204-5d71-9b6f-bfec8ffb9b3c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317017}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab973c13-19dd-5e07-b659-ecb6595e1561" :question/math-academy-id "q-128289" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=-{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+24x-7.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-3x^2-6x+24$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nIf $x={x}_{1}$ and $x={x}_{2}$ are the critical points of $f(x),$ what is the value of ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-8$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nFind the values of $x$ for which the function $f(x)$ is increasing.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ is increasing for $x\\,\\in \\,($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,,\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,)$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nFor which values of $x$ does the function $f(x)$ have local maxima and local minima?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \nis a local minimum\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $x\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \nis a local maximum\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nWhat are the global maximum and global minimum values of $f(x)$ on the interval $[-1,3]?$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The global maximum is ${f}_{max}\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $21$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The global minimum is ${f}_{min}\\,=\\,$  \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-33$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{″}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{″}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-6x-6$\n\nEarlier part 7:\n\nDetermine the largest interval on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $x\\,\\in \\,($ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\infty$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$\\,,\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         \n$)$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nFind the $x$-coordinate of the point of inflection for the function $f(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The point of inflection is at $x\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Earlier, we found that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x) & =-6x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the points of inflection, we solve ${f}^{″}(x)=0.$ This gives\n\n$$\n-6x-6=0\\,⟹\\,x=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we need to check that the concavity changes at $x=-1.$ To do that, we take two points, one to the left of $x=-1$ and another to the right of $x=-1.$ \n\nFor example, let's take the points $x=-2$ and $x=0.$ We compute the sign of ${f}^{″}(x)$ at the selected points:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2) & =-6(-2)-6=6>0 \\\\ {f}^{″}(0) & =-6(0)-6=-6<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the second derivative changes sign at $x=-1.$ \n\nSince the concavity changes at $x=-1,$ we conclude that $x=-1$ is indeed a point of inflection."}]}
